From 402eab7230506e8d52addb8c1455e536f2dfff05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: superc <superc@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 20:32:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 9/10] superc: struct methods called as 'p->f(...)'

'ret f(struct T *self, ...)' is registered as a method of T, and the
call syntax 'p->f(a)' is rewritten into 'f(p, a)'.  The function itself
is untouched: it keeps its symbol name and its calling convention, so
'fflush(FILE *)' or 'gettimeofday(struct timeval *)' are not renamed
and libc keeps working, and a method is still callable as a plain
function.

Methods are registered for every file-scope prototype or definition
whose first parameter points at a struct (or union), including ones
declared inside a package: the registry keeps the name as written, so
'package shape { int area(Vec *v) }' is reachable as 'v->area()' and
as 'shape.area(v)'.  Two methods with the same name for the same type
are rejected as duplicates; different types may share a name when they
live in different packages, since a plain C function name is unique.

A real field of the same name keeps plain C semantics, only '->' is a
method call, and the receiver must be a pointer.  The hidden receiver
is pushed after a possible structure-return buffer, which also fixes
the argument order for interface methods returning a structure.

Tests: 169 methods (including a structure return, an incomplete type,
a packaged method and a 'T!error' method with try/catch), 170 their
diagnostics.  tests2 151/151 with TCC_EXT.
